absolutely ridiculous  a month-long shortage of baby formula in  the u.s caused by a mix of supply chain  issues and a recall is putting desperate  parents on edge as they scramble to find  ways to feed their babies  for those babies who are not being  breastfed or otherwise getting human  milk formula is only the alternative and  it provides all of their nutrition  so what can parents do now and more  importantly what should they avoid  definitely do not wet it down or make  your own formula these are dangerous and  there's simply no safe way to do those  dr stephen abrams a former chair of the  committee on nutrition at the american  academy of pediatrics says that similar  to breast milk baby formula contains  protein and nutrients that are crucial  to a child's development and growth such  as calcium phosphorus iron and zinc  when you make your own you can't get the  nutrients right it's hard to do it in a  way that's sterile enough to make sure  that you're not risking infection  when you dilute the formula you're  simply giving the baby extra water that  they don't need and they're filling up  their tummies with weather and that food  it's what they need to grow  it's okay to switch baby formulas unless  your baby has special medical needs  experts say it's safe for most healthy  babies to switch to any fda approved  formula parents can find from reputable  distributors the american academy of  pediatrics warns against buying formulas  on auction sites that are not monitored  or regulated by the fda  babies have specialized problems  especially those with severe allergies  families need to talk to the  pediatrician before they switch  as major retailers like cvs walgreens  kroger and target impose purchase limits  on baby formula to avoid panic buying  and hoarding products experts recommend  checking smaller local stores and  pharmacies i've seen it for example in  stores that only sell kosher food  because they make specialized kosher  formula sometimes you'll find it in  smaller stores that you might not expect  it to find it in  at least 31 milk banks in the u.s and  canada are collecting donations from  lactating women and distributing  pasteurized milk these members of the  human milk banking association of north  america are currently helping families  facing difficulties due to the formula  shortage the problem is that most of  that milk goes for premature babies  there isn't a whole lot left for the  general use but it's as long as the milk  is pasteurized it's quite safe  although the cdc says parents who want  to give their children cow's milk should  wait until after the infant's first  birthday dr abrams says this can be a  last resort option for babies older than  six months in case of an emergency for  short periods of time a week two weeks  of time you can use whole cow milk if  your baby's not allergic  dr abrams says parents should talk to  their pediatricians about getting an  iron supplement for babies who are on  cow's milk because whole cow milk is too  low in iron for the nutritional needs  the shortage which stems from  pandemic-related supply issues worsened  after abbott laboratories a major  formula manufacturer voluntarily  recalled some products and closed a  plant  on may 16 the company announced that it  had reached a deal with the fda for the  reopening of their formula plant but a  date has not yet been confirmed  on may 18th president biden invoked the  defense production act to help  manufacturers increase production of  baby formula the administration also  launched a new program that would use  commercial aircrafts to pick up overseas  formula that meets u.s standards i'm  hopeful that the government allowing  imports and increased production will  begin to see a turnaround over the next  couple weeks but i think we're still  going to need to be very careful i don't  expect to see full resolution until  really  multiple months from now and families  need to be aware of that
 

